{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-46507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The expression $-100-36{m}^{2}$ is equivalent to" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-46507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's factor out $-4$ from the expression. We have\n\n$$\n-100-36{m}^{2}=-4(25+9{m}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet's now focus on factoring the expression $(25+9{m}^{2}).$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25+9{m}^{2} & =25-(-9{m}^{2}) \\\\  & =25-(9{\\text{i}}^{2}{m}^{2}) \\\\  & ={5}^{2}-{(3\\text{i}m)}^{2} \\\\  & =(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into the expression, we get\n\n$$\n-4(25+9{m}^{2})=-4(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-46507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bc381fd6d845c7e91c8235ec7424fb5421d4dfc8b839f75c27c5584c280ba69"} {"question" "q-46507" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-46507" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-(3+5\\text{i}m)(3-5\\text{i}m)"] ["math" "-(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)"] ["math" "-4(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)"] ["math" "-5(3+4\\text{i}m)(3-4\\text{i}m)"] ["math" "-5(4+3\\text{i}m)(4-3\\text{i}m)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-46507" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4(5+3\\text{i}m)(5-3\\text{i}m)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-87322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Factor ${y}^{10}+16.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-87322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n{y}^{10}+16={({y}^{5})}^{2}+{4}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{10}+16 & ={y}^{10}-(-16) \\\\  & ={y}^{10}-(16{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={({y}^{5})}^{2}-{(4\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}^{5}+4\\text{i})({y}^{5}-4\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-87322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bc381fd6d845c7e91c8235ec7424fb5421d4dfc8b839f75c27c5584c280ba69"} {"question" "q-87322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-87322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "({y}^{10}+4)({y}^{10}-4)"] ["math" "({y}^{5}+4\\text{i})({y}^{5}-4\\text{i})"] ["math" "({y}^{5}+8\\text{i})({y}^{5}-8\\text{i})"] ["math" "{({y}^{5}+4\\text{i})}^{2}"] ["math" "{({y}^{5}-8\\text{i})}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-87322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "({y}^{5}+4\\text{i})({y}^{5}-4\\text{i})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-170502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Factor $4{w}^{4}+36{w}^{2}+81.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-170502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${w}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{4}+36{w}^{2}+81 & ={(2{w}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (2{w}^{2})\\cdot 9+{9}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{w}^{2}+9)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{w}^{2}+9 & =2{w}^{2}-(-9) \\\\  & =2{w}^{2}-(9{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}w)^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{4}+36{w}^{2}+81 & =(2{w}^{2}+9)^{2} \\\\  & =((\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i}))^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-170502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bc381fd6d845c7e91c8235ec7424fb5421d4dfc8b839f75c27c5584c280ba69"} {"question" "q-170502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-170502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(\\sqrt{2}w+3)^{2}(\\sqrt{2}w-3)^{2}"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i})^{2}"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})^{4}"] ["math" "4(w+3)^{2}(w-3)^{2}"] ["math" "4(w+3\\text{i})^{2}(w-3\\text{i})^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-170502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\sqrt{2}w+3\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{2}w-3\\text{i})^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-320782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write $-12{t}^{2}-108$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$-12{t}^{2}-108=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-320782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's factor out $-3$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n-12{t}^{2}-108=-3\\,(4{t}^{2}+36).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(4{t}^{2}+36).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2}+36 & =4{t}^{2}-(-36) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-(36{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(2t)^{2}-(6i)^{2} \\\\  & =(2t+6i)(2t-6i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3\\,(4{t}^{2}+36)=-3(2t+6i)(2t-6i).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-320782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bc381fd6d845c7e91c8235ec7424fb5421d4dfc8b839f75c27c5584c280ba69"} {"question" "q-320782" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-320782" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-12(t+3i)(t-3i)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-367716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write $81{y}^{4}+72{y}^{2}+16$ in factored form as $(a+bi)^{2}(a-bi)^{2}.$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$81{y}^{4}+72{y}^{2}+16=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-367716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${y}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{y}^{4}+72{y}^{2}+16 & ={(9{y}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (9{y}^{2})\\cdot 4+{4}^{2} \\\\  & ={(9{y}^{2}+4)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}+4 & =9{y}^{2}-(-4) \\\\  & =9{y}^{2}-4({i}^{2}) \\\\  & ={(3y)}^{2}-{(2i)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3y+2i)(3y-2i)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{y}^{4}+72{y}^{2}+16 & ={(9{y}^{2}+4)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((3y+2i)(3y-2i))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(3y+2i)}^{2}{(3y-2i)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-367716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bc381fd6d845c7e91c8235ec7424fb5421d4dfc8b839f75c27c5584c280ba69"} {"question" "q-367716" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"} {"question" "q-367716" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3y+2i)^2(3y-2i)^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973465/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c04154d8837fce44c7b06402e1069785024c6033dd271a3a6bed9e3d6e0dfa"}] "usage" {} "basis" 670 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
